## Solution

**Setup.** Let $x_1, x_2, x_3 \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. The $3\times 3$ Vandermonde matrix is

$$V = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x_1 & x_1^2 \\ 1 & x_2 & x_2^2 \\ 1 & x_3 & x_3^2 \end{pmatrix},$$

with determinant

$$\det(V) = \prod_{1 \le i < j \le 3}(x_j - x_i) = (x_2 - x_1)(x_3 - x_1)(x_3 - x_2).$$

We seek $E[\det(V)]$.

**Symmetry argument.** The joint distribution of $(x_1, x_2, x_3)$ is invariant under the negation map $(x_1, x_2, x_3) \mapsto (-x_1, -x_2, -x_3)$, since each $x_i$ is i.i.d. standard normal (a distribution symmetric about $0$).

Under this map, each factor of the determinant transforms as

$$x_j - x_i \;\mapsto\; (-x_j) - (-x_i) = -(x_j - x_i).$$

The determinant is a product of $\binom{3}{2} = 3$ such factors, so

$$\det(V) \;\mapsto\; (-1)^3 \det(V) = -\det(V).$$

Thus $\det(V)$ is an **odd function** of $(x_1, x_2, x_3)$ under simultaneous negation.

**Computing the expectation.** By the invariance of the joint distribution under negation,

$$E[\det(V)] = E\big[\det(V)\big|_{(x_1,x_2,x_3)}\big] = E\big[\det(V)\big|_{(-x_1,-x_2,-x_3)}\big] = E[-\det(V)].$$

Hence

$$E[\det(V)] = -E[\det(V)] \implies 2\,E[\det(V)] = 0 \implies E[\det(V)] = 0.$$

**Conclusion.** The expected value of the determinant is

$$\boxed{E\big[\det(V)\big] = 0}.$$

*Remark.* This argument generalizes: for an $n\times n$ Vandermonde matrix with i.i.d. nodes from any distribution symmetric about $0$, the determinant is a product of $\binom{n}{2}$ differences, each odd under negation, so $\det(V) \mapsto (-1)^{\binom{n}{2}}\det(V)$. The expectation vanishes whenever $\binom{n}{2}$ is odd, i.e. whenever $n \equiv 2$ or $3 \pmod 4$. For $n=3$, $\binom{3}{2}=3$ is odd, giving the result above.

